WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
The game Wordle has gone pretty viral in the&nbsp;
last month or two, and never one to overlook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.320
an opportunity for a math lesson, it occurs to&nbsp;
me that this game makes for a very good central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.720
example in a lesson about information theory,&nbsp;
and in particular a topic known as entropy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.760
You see like a lot of people I got kind of sucked&nbsp;
into the puzzle, and like a lot of programmers I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.040
also got sucked into trying to write an algorithm&nbsp;
that would play the game as optimally as it could.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.720
What I thought I'd do here is just talk through&nbsp;
with you some of my process in that, and explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.040
some of the math that went into it, since the&nbsp;
whole algorithm centers on this idea of entropy.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.120
First things first, in case you haven't heard of&nbsp;
it, what is Wordle and to kill two birds with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.960
stone here while we go through the rules of the&nbsp;
game let me also preview where we're going with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.800
this, which is to develop a little algorithm&nbsp;
that will basically play the game for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.360
I haven't done today's Wordle, this is&nbsp;
February 4th, and we'll see how the bot does.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.200
The goal of Wordle is to guess a mystery&nbsp;
five-letter word, and you're given six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.920
different chances to guess. For example, my&nbsp;
wordlebot suggests that I start with the guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.120
"crane". Each time that you make a guess, you&nbsp;
get some information about how close your guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.240
is to the true answer. Here the gray box is&nbsp;
telling me there's no c in the actual answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.080
the yellow box is telling me there is&nbsp;
an r but it's not in that position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.560
The green box is telling me that the secret word&nbsp;
does have an a and it's in the third position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.800
And then there's no 'n' and there's no 'e'. Let&nbsp;
me just go in and tell the wordlebot about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.240
information...we started with "crane",&nbsp;
we got gray yellow green grey grey...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.720
Don't worry about all the data that it's showing&nbsp;
right now, I'll explain that in due time.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.680
Its top suggestion for our second pick is "shtik".&nbsp;
Your guess does have to be an actual five-letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.280
word, but as you'll see it's pretty liberal&nbsp;
with what it will actually let you guess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.560
In this case we try stick and...all right!&nbsp;
Things are looking pretty good. We hit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.840
's' and the 'h', so we know the first three&nbsp;
letters, and we know that there's an 'r'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.160
So it's going to be like s-h-a something r&nbsp;
or s-h-a-r-something. And it looks like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.160
Wordle-bot knows that it's down to just two&nbsp;
possibilities, either "shard" or "sharp".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.560
I's kind of a toss-up between them at this&nbsp;
point, so I guess probably just because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.680
alphabetical it goes with shard, which...hooray!&nbsp;
It is the actual answer. So we got it in three.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.160
If you're wondering if that's any good, the way&nbsp;
I heard one person phrase it is that with Wordle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.720
four is par and three is birdie,&nbsp;
which I think is a pretty apt analogy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.280
You have to be consistently on your game to&nbsp;
be getting four but it's certainly not crazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.760
But when you get it in three, it just feels great.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.000
If you're down for it what I'd like to do here&nbsp;
is just talk through my thought process from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.160
beginning for how I approach the wordlebot.&nbsp;
And like I said really it's an excuse for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.720
information theory lesson, the main goal is to&nbsp;
explain what is information and what is entropy.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.240
My first thought in approaching this was to take&nbsp;
a look at the relative frequencies of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.480
letters in the english language. I thought,&nbsp;
okay, is there an opening guess or an opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.880
pair of guesses that hits a lot of these most&nbsp;
frequent letters. One that I was pretty fond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.440
of was doing "other" followed by "nails". The&nbsp;
thought is that if you hit a letter, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.200
you get a green or a yellow, that always feels&nbsp;
good, it feels like you're getting information.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.320
But in these cases even if you&nbsp;
don't hit and you always get greys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.840
that's still giving you a lot of information,&nbsp;
since it's pretty rare to find a word that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.320
doesn't have any of these letters. But even&nbsp;
still that doesn't feel super systematic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.240
because for example it does nothing to consider&nbsp;
the order of the letters. Why type "nails" when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.320
I could type "snail". Is it better to have&nbsp;
that s at the end? I'm not really sure.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.320
Now a friend of mine said that he&nbsp;
liked to open with the word "weary",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.840
which kind of surprised me because it has&nbsp;
some uncommon letters in there like the 'w'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.840
and the 'y'. But who knows, maybe that&nbsp;
is a better opener. Is there some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.160
of quantitative score that we can give to&nbsp;
judge the quality of a potential guess?

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.040
To set up for the way that we're going to rank&nbsp;
possible guesses, let's go back and add a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.000
clarity to how exactly the game is set up. There's&nbsp;
a list of words that it will allow you to enter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.640
that are considered valid guesses, that's just&nbsp;
about 13,000 words long. But when you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.440
it there's a lot of really uncommon things&nbsp;
things like "aahed" or "aalii" and "aargh".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.720
The kind of words that bring about&nbsp;
family arguments in a game of Scrabble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.800
But the vibe of the game is that the answer&nbsp;
is always going to be a decently common word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.480
and in fact there's another list of around 2,300&nbsp;
words that are the possible answers. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.520
human-curated list, I think specifically by the&nbsp;
game creator's girlfriend which is kind of fun.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.360
But what I would like to do, our challenge&nbsp;
for this project, is to see if we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.480
write a program solving Wordle that doesn't&nbsp;
incorporate previous knowledge about this list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.640
For one thing there's plenty of pretty common five&nbsp;
letter words that you won't find in that list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.320
so it would be better to write a program that's&nbsp;
a little more resilient and would play Wordle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.880
against anyone, not just what happens to be&nbsp;
the official website. And also, the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.200
that we know what this list of possible answers&nbsp;
is is because it's visible in the source code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.840
but the way that it's visible in the source code&nbsp;
is in the specific order in which answers come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.800
from day to day, so you could always just look up&nbsp;
what tomorrow's answer will be. So clearly there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.720
some sense in which using the list is cheating,&nbsp;
and what makes for a more interesting puzzle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.760
a richer information theory lesson is to instead&nbsp;
use some more universal data, like relative word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.320
frequencies in general, to capture this intuition&nbsp;
of having a preference for more common words.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.240
So! Of these 13,000 possibilities, how&nbsp;
should we choose the opening guess?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.720
For example if my friend proposes "weary", how&nbsp;
should we analyze its quality? Well the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.760
he said he likes that unlikely 'w' is that he&nbsp;
likes the long shot nature of just how good it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.640
feels if you do hit that 'w'. For example if the&nbsp;
first pattern revealed was something like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.840
then it turns out there are only 58 words in&nbsp;
this giant lexicon that match that pattern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.120
so that's a huge reduction from 13,000. But the&nbsp;
flip side of that, of course, is that it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.480
uncommon to get a pattern like this. Specifically,&nbsp;
if each word was equally likely to be the answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.400
the probability of hitting this pattern&nbsp;
would be 58 divided by around 13,000.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.040
Of course, they're not equally likely to be&nbsp;
answers, most of these are very obscure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.880
even questionable words, but at least for our&nbsp;
first pass at all of this let's assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.120
they're all equally likely, then refine that a&nbsp;
bit later. The point is the pattern with a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.600
of information is by its very nature unlikely to&nbsp;
occur. In fact what it means to be informative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.480
is that it's unlikely. A much more&nbsp;
probable pattern to see with this opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.040
would be something like this, where of course&nbsp;
there's not a 'w' in it, maybe there's an 'e'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.600
and maybe there's no 'a', there's no 'r', and&nbsp;
there's no 'y'. In this case there are 1,400&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.200
possible matches. So if all were equally likely,&nbsp;
it works out to be a probability of about 11% that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:34.000
this is the pattern you would see. So the most&nbsp;
likely outcomes are also the least informative.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.320
To get a more global view here, let me show&nbsp;
you the full distribution of probabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.560
across all of the different&nbsp;
patterns that you might see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.880
Each bar that you're looking at corresponds to a&nbsp;
possible pattern of colors that could be revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.960
of which there are 3^5 possibilities.&nbsp;
And they're organized from left to right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.920
most common to least common. So the most common&nbsp;
possibility here is that you get all grays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.120
that happens about 14% of the time. What you're&nbsp;
hoping for when you make a guess is that you end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.680
up somewhere out in this long tail, like over&nbsp;
here where there's only 18 possibilities for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.960
what matches this pattern, that evidently look&nbsp;
like this. Or if we venture a little farther&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:16.320
to the left...you know maybe we go all the way&nbsp;
over here...okay here's a good puzzle for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.800
What are the three words in the english language&nbsp;
that start with a 'w' end with a 'y' and have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.640
'r' somewhere in them? It turns out the answers&nbsp;
are... let's see... "wordy" "wormy" and "wrily".

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.200
To judge how good this word is overall, we want&nbsp;
some kind of measure of the expected amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.520
information that you're going to get from this&nbsp;
distribution. If we go through each pattern and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.720
we multiply its probability of occurring times&nbsp;
something that measures how informative it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.520
that can maybe give us an objective score. Now&nbsp;
your first instinct for what that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.720
should be might be the number of matches, you&nbsp;
know you want a lower average number of matches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.120
but instead I'd like to use a more universal&nbsp;
measurement that we often ascribe to information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.400
and one that will be more flexible&nbsp;
once we have a different probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.160
assigned to each of these 13,000 words for&nbsp;
whether or not they're actually the answer.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.560
The standard unit of information is the bit,&nbsp;
which has a little bit of a funny formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.880
but it's really intuitive&nbsp;
if we just look at examples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.360
If you have an observation that cuts&nbsp;
your space of possibilities in half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.680
we say that it has one bit of information.&nbsp;
In our example the space of possibilities is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.600
all possible words, and it turns out about&nbsp;
half of the five letter words have an 's',&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.240
a little less than that but about half. So that&nbsp;
observation would give you one bit of information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.840
If instead a new fact chops down that space of&nbsp;
possibilities by a factor of four, we say that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.320
has two bits of information. For example it turns&nbsp;
out about a quarter of these words have a 't'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.360
If the observation cuts that space by a factor of&nbsp;
eight, we say it has three bits of information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.840
and so on and so forth. Four bits cuts it into&nbsp;
a sixteenth, five bits cuts it into a 32nd.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.960
So now is when you might want to take a moment to&nbsp;
pause and ask for yourself, what is the formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.960
for information, for the number of bits in&nbsp;
terms of the probability of an occurrence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.440
Well, what we're saying here is basically that&nbsp;
when you take one half to the number of bits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.600
that's the same thing as the probability, which&nbsp;
is the same thing as saying 2 to the power of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.520
number of bits is 1 over the probability,&nbsp;
which rearranges further to saying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.720
information is the log base 2 of 1 divided by the&nbsp;
probability. And sometimes you see this with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.800
more rearrangement still, where the information&nbsp;
is the negative log base 2 of the probability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.200
Expressed like this it can look a little bit&nbsp;
weird to the uninitiated, but it really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.920
just the very intuitive idea of asking how many&nbsp;
times you've cut down your possibilities in half.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.920
Now if you're wondering, you know, I thought&nbsp;
we were just playing a fun word game why are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.240
logarithms entering the picture? One reason this&nbsp;
is a nicer unit is it just a lot easier to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.120
about very unlikely events. Much easier to say&nbsp;
that an observation has 20 bits of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.120
than it is to say that the probability&nbsp;
of such and such occurring is 0.00000095.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.640
But a more substantive reason that this&nbsp;
logarithmic expression turned out to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.920
very useful addition to the theory of probability&nbsp;
is the way that information adds together. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.480
example if one observation gives you two bits&nbsp;
of information, cutting your space down by four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.880
and then a second observation, like your second&nbsp;
guess in Wordle, gives you another three bits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.280
of information, chopping you down further by&nbsp;
another factor of eight, the two together give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.520
you five bits of information. In the same&nbsp;
way that probabilities like to multiply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:24.320
Information likes to add. So as soon as we're in&nbsp;
the realm of something like an expected value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.760
where we're adding a bunch of numbers up,&nbsp;
the logs make it a lot nicer to deal with.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.240
Let's go back to our distribution for weary&nbsp;
and add another little tracker on here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.800
showing us how much information&nbsp;
there is for each pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.080
The main thing I want you to notice&nbsp;
is that the higher the probability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.640
as we get to those more likely patterns, the&nbsp;
lower the information, the fewer bits you gain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:48.320
The way we measure the quality of this guess will&nbsp;
be to take the expected value of this information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.080
where we go through each pattern, we say how&nbsp;
probable is it, and then we multiply that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.240
how many bits of information do we get. And in the&nbsp;
example of weary, that turns out to be 4.9 bits.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:02.400
So on average, the information you get&nbsp;
from this opening guess is as good as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.480
chopping your space of possibilities&nbsp;
in half about five times. By contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.600
an example of a guess with a higher expected&nbsp;
information value would be something like "slate".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.040
In this case you'll notice the distribution&nbsp;
looks a lot flatter, in particular the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:22.480
probable occurrence of all grays only has about&nbsp;
a 6% chance of occurring. So at minimum you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:27.280
getting, evidently, 3.9 bits of information.&nbsp;
But that's a minimum, more typically you'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:31.280
get something better than that. And it turns out&nbsp;
when you crunch the numbers on this one and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:38.240
add up all of the relevant terms, the average&nbsp;
information is about 5.8. So in contrast with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.440
weary your space of possibilities will be about&nbsp;
half as big after this first guess, on average.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.640
There's actually a fun story about the name for&nbsp;
this expected value of information quantity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.320
You see information theory was developed by&nbsp;
Claude Shannon, who was working at Bell labs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:57.360
in the 1940s. He was talking about some of his&nbsp;
yet-to-be-published ideas with John von Neumann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.440
who was this intellectual giant of the time,&nbsp;
a very prominent in math and physics and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.800
beginnings of what was becoming computer&nbsp;
science. And when he mentioned that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:08.640
didn't really have a good name for this&nbsp;
expected value of information quantity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:13.120
von Neumann supposedly said, so the story&nbsp;
goes, "well you should call it Entropy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.600
and for two reasons. In the first place your&nbsp;
uncertainty function has been used in statistical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.640
mechanics under that name, so it already has a&nbsp;
name. And in the second place, and more important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.720
nobody knows what entropy really is, so in&nbsp;
a debate you'll always have the advantage."

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:31.120
So if the name seems a little bit mysterious,&nbsp;
and if this story is to be believed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.680
that's kind of by design. Also, if you're&nbsp;
wondering about its relation to all of that second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.240
law of thermodynamics stuff from physics, there&nbsp;
definitely is a connection, but in its origins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.560
Shannon was just dealing with pure probability&nbsp;
theory. And for our purposes here, when I use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:49.440
the word entropy, I just want you to think the&nbsp;
expected information value of a particular guess.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.160
You can think of entropy as&nbsp;
measuring two things simultaneously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:59.440
The first one is how flat is the distribution.&nbsp;
The closer a distribution is to uniform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.400
the higher that entropy will be. In our&nbsp;
case, where there are 3^5 total patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:09.200
for a uniform distribution, observing any one&nbsp;
of them would have information log_2(3^5),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:16.320
which happens to be 7.92. So that is the&nbsp;
absolute maximum that you could possibly have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:21.360
for this entropy. But entropy is also kind of a&nbsp;
measure of how many possibilities there are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:26.720
the first place. For example if you happen to have&nbsp;
some word where there's only 16 possible patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:32.720
and each one is equally likely, this entropy, this&nbsp;
expected information, would be four bits. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:36.960
you have another word where there are 64 possible&nbsp;
patterns that could come up, and they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:42.800
equally likely, then the entropy would work out to&nbsp;
be six bits. So if you see some distribution out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:48.560
in the wild that has an entropy of six bits, it's&nbsp;
sort of like it's saying there's as much variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:53.440
and uncertainty in what's about to happen&nbsp;
as if there were 64 equally likely outcomes.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:58.160
For my first pass at the worldbot,&nbsp;
I basically had it just do this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:01.360
It goes through all of the different&nbsp;
possible guesses that you could have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:07.120
all 13,000 words. It computes the entropy for&nbsp;
each one, or more specifically the entropy of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:12.080
distribution across all patterns that you might&nbsp;
see for each one, and then it picks the highest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:15.840
since that's the one that's likely to chop down&nbsp;
your space of possibilities as much as possible.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:20.240
